55,3,,status,Toshio,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":4,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
56,3,,status,Yumi,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":5,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
57,3,1,talk,Asuka,,"I lean toward Candidate A. It addresses the readmission issues I found in the call-back trial and avoids the physical space constraints Toshio mentioned, as it takes place in the patient's home.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
58,3,1,talk,Toshio,,"I find Candidate A promising because it bypasses our parking and room shortages. However, we must ensure the coordination is logged properly, or it will become the invisible workload I fear.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
59,3,1,talk,Benjamin,,"I must cast a skeptical eye on Candidate A. While it avoids the parking lot, it invites the ""locked gates"" and weather delays I discovered. It is a lovely idea that may simply vanish into the rain.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
60,3,1,talk,Yumi,,"I think Candidate B is a wonderful choice! It uses the slack in the midwifery rota I found, and it would be so lovely to support new parents and their babies!","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
61,3,1,talk,Servas,,"Candidate B is a heartwarming prospect, Yumi, and it utilizes our existing strengths. However, we must remember that on-site sessions are our only source of revenue. A program that brings people into our already full rooms is a risk.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[""Yumi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
62,3,2,talk,Asuka,,"@Yumi, while Candidate B is appealing, it is a joint program between midwifery and pediatrics. My research into the board minutes shows that joint clinics often collapse due to budget and rota disputes. It is too risky.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Yumi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
63,3,2,talk,Toshio,,"@Yumi, Candidate B would exacerbate the parking and room shortages I discovered. Adding more scheduled consultations to a system that is already full by mid-morning is a recipe for total congestion.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[""Yumi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
64,3,2,talk,Benjamin,,"@Asuka, your caution is well-placed. Candidate B is a charming vision, but as a joint venture, it is likely to become a battleground for departmental budgets once the initial enthusiasm fades. It is a fragile bridge.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
65,3,2,talk,Yumi,,"@Asuka, @Toshio, I see what you mean! I didn't think about the parking or the budget fights. I just want everyone to be happy! Maybe Candidate D could work? It helps people with many conditions!","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Asuka"",""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,2,talk,Servas,,"@Yumi, your kindness is a gift. Candidate D is an intriguing path, as it focuses on coordination. However, we must ask if this coordination happens within our walls or through those fragile external agreements Benjamin mentioned.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Yumi"",""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,3,talk,Asuka,,"@Servas, Candidate D relies heavily on communication with providers outside the hospital. Given Benjamin's findings on the administrative marathon of external data agreements, D is likely to stall before it ever helps a patient.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Servas"",""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,3,talk,Toshio,,"@Asuka, I agree. Candidate D's reliance on external providers and consolidated appointments would likely overload our booking system, which already fails to track non-fixed slots. It risks becoming a bureaucratic bottleneck.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,3,talk,Benjamin,,"@Asuka, you've hit the nail on the head. Candidate D is essentially a request for a dozen signatures from outside surgeries—a bureaucratic odyssey that would leave our coordinators staring at blank forms for fourteen months. It is a fantasy.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
70,3,3,talk,Yumi,,"Oh dear, it seems like almost every option has a scary problem! I really don't want our staff to be stressed. If D is too much paperwork, maybe A is the best way to help people without filling up the parking lot?","{""words"":42,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
71,3,3,talk,Servas,,"@Yumi, your desire to protect our staff is a noble guide. While Candidate A avoids the parking lot, we must remember that off-site visits draw no regional fund tariffs. We must be careful not to create a service that drains us.","{""words"":41,""mentions"":[""Yumi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
72,3,4,talk,Asuka,,"Despite the lack of tariffs, Candidate A is the only option that avoids the fatal flaws of the others: it bypasses the space crisis, avoids joint-departmental collapse, and sidesteps external bureaucracy. It is the most sustainable choice.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
73,3,4,talk,Toshio,,"I concur. While the lack of tariffs is a concern for Servas, Candidate A is the only proposal that does not threaten to seize up our physical infrastructure or collapse under bureaucratic weight. I vote for Candidate A.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""Servas""],""raw_differs"":false}"
74,3,4,talk,Benjamin,,"While I maintain my reservations about the rain and the locked gates, Candidate A is indeed the least offensive of our options. It is a modest victory of avoidance over catastrophe. I vote for Candidate A.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
75,3,4,talk,Yumi,,"I'll vote for Candidate A too! Even if it's not perfect, it's the best way to help our patients without making our wonderful staff feel even more overwhelmed. Let's go with A!","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
76,3,4,talk,Servas,,"It is a lesson in humility to accept the least imperfect path. While the lack of tariffs is a burden, the risk of a total system collapse is a far heavier price. I vote for Candidate A.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
